{"index":3,"id":"R004","argument":[{"id":"S001","text":"Fix sigma,tau,t0 and polynomial inputs. In each nonzero term write k=a+b+c, p=a+b, d=-k, A0=|ab|,B0=|cd|, and let N3 be the third largest of |a|,|b|,|c|,|d|. Terms with a zero input frequency, zero k, or zero phase vanish by definition and N002. On the remaining terms alpha=3abp,beta=3kcp. The ratio bound of N002 and the first majorant of N003 give |eta|/|k|<=6/N3. The small-phase bound gives |eta|/(tau|abck|)<=3|p|min(A0,B0)/(A0*B0)=3|p|/max(A0,B0)<=18/N3 by N003. Their geometric interpolation with weights 1-sigma,sigma yields |eta|/(|k||abc|^sigma)<=6^(1-sigma)18^sigma*tau^sigma/N3. This includes sigma=0 and sigma=1 as the separate endpoint bounds."},{"id":"S002","text":"Dualize the Fourier coefficients of Rem against a finitely supported unit ell^2 sequence. After absolute values, use F001 and N003 with x_1(a)=|a|^sigma|hat f_1(a)| and likewise for slots 2 and 3; take x_4(d) to be the modulus of the dual coefficient at -d. Their norms are bounded by the three H^sigma norms and the unit dual norm. The factor exp(-i*t0*Phi) has modulus one, and the prefactor in Rem is tau/18. Thus ||Rem||_2<=C_sigma*tau^(1+sigma)product_j||f_j||_(H^sigma). A multiplier of modulus <=1 only decreases the absolute majorant, so the same proof covers every stated restriction."},{"id":"S003","text":"Approximate each mean-zero H^sigma input by its finite Fourier projections. The trilinear bound F002 and the three-term telescoping difference bound show that the outputs are Cauchy in the complete L^2 space, independently of the chosen approximations. This defines a unique bounded trilinear extension and retains the inequality. For smooth inputs it coincides with the original absolutely convergent Fourier expression. At sigma=0 this supplies in particular an L^2-by-L^2-by-L^2 bound C*tau."},{"id":"S004","text":"For the final clause fix f,w,M0 and temporarily assume L1: 0<sigma<=1,0<tau<=1,w in mean-zero H^sigma with ||w||_(H^sigma)<=M0, f in mean-zero L^2, and delta=||f-w||_2<=1. Apply F003 with exponent sigma to (w,w,w). Expand Rem(f,f,f)-Rem(w,w,w) as Rem(f-w,f,f)+Rem(w,f-w,f)+Rem(w,w,f-w) and apply F003 with exponent zero to each term. The difference is bounded by C*tau*delta*(||f||_2+||w||_2)^2. Since ||f||_2<=M0+1, this yields C_(sigma,M0)*tau*(tau^sigma+delta). Discharge L1 and quantify all data. This proves the output assertion."}]}
{"index":4,"id":"R005","argument":[{"id":"S001","text":"For any mean-zero L^2 f, Cauchy-Schwarz in its Fourier series gives ||Dinv f||_infinity<=(sum_(k!=0)k^(-2))^(1/2)||f||_2, and coefficient weights give ||Dinv f||_(H^1)<=sqrt(2)||f||_2. For a general H^1 function its mean plus this estimate for its derivative gives ||A0||_infinity<=C||A0||_(H^1). The product rule then gives ||A0*B0||_(H^1)<=C||A0||_(H^1)||B0||_(H^1). Apply it to S_t Dinv f and S_t Dinv g and use S_t isometric and P contractive to obtain ||J_t(f,g)||_(H^1)<=C||f||_2||g||_2. Each assertion is quantified over inputs in its indicated space."},{"id":"S002","text":"For L^2 f,g, every Fourier coefficient of the product (S_t f)(S_t g) has modulus <=||f||_2||g||_2 by Cauchy-Schwarz in the convolution. Hence ||B_t(f,g)||_(H^(-pstar))^2<=||f||_2^2||g||_2^2 sum_(k!=0) k^2<k>^(-2*pstar), a finite sum because pstar=23/14>3/2. The same coefficient bound with sum k^(-2) proves ||Dinv P(f*g)||_2<=C||f||_2||g||_2. For mean-zero A0, <k>^(-pstar)<=|k|^(-1), so ||A0||_(H^(-pstar))<=||Dinv A0||_2. Finally J_t(A0,g) is a product of S_t Dinv A0 in L^2 and S_t Dinv g in L^infinity, followed by contractions, so its L^2 norm is <=C||Dinv A0||_2||g||_2 by F001. Approximation by polynomials and these continuous bilinear bounds justify the products and their extensions."},{"id":"S003","text":"For the normal-form clause take smooth mean-zero paths f,g on [a,b]. At k!=0 the coefficient of J_t is sum_(r+s=k) exp(-i*t*3*k*r*s)*(i*r)^(-1)*(i*s)^(-1)*hat f(r)*hat g(s). Differentiating the phase gives 3*i*k times the product coefficients. Therefore d_t J_t(f,g)=3*B_t(f,g)+J_t(f',g)+J_t(f,g'). Smooth spatial Fourier series and all derivatives used here converge absolutely, so differentiation and integration are legitimate. Integration gives the displayed two-endpoint normal form. For piecewise smooth continuous paths apply this on each subinterval; intermediate J endpoint values cancel because the paths are continuous."},{"id":"S004","text":"Symmetry of J is immediate from the product formula. Since Dinv partial_x=P, substitution gives J_t(B_t(f,g),h)=S_(-t)P{P[(S_t f)(S_t g)]*(S_t Dinv h)}, whose integral on I is exactly Ctri_I(f,g,h); no inverse derivative is commuted through a product. For constant f,g, integrate F003 and multiply by 1/2 to obtain Fstage_(a,t)(f,g)=(1/6)[J_s(f,g)]_a^t. This establishes every identity asserted in the output assertion."}]}
{"index":5,"id":"R006","argument":[{"id":"S001","text":"Apply E003 on I=[a,b]. For its first assertion take alpha=0 and any slot i in {1,2,3}. The stated spatially smooth paths, continuous and piecewise smooth in time on a finite compact partition, have bounded L2 norms and bounded piecewise H^(-pstar) derivatives. Integration by parts against a compactly supported smooth scalar time test function on each piece gives their weak derivatives; adjacent endpoint terms cancel by continuity, so there are no jump masses. Thus all three paths satisfy E003's hypotheses. For its second assertion take alpha=sigma, with arbitrary 0<=sigma<=1 and constant H^sigma inputs; their weak derivatives are zero. Put c0=sqrt(2*pi). Every source spatial norm and source X norm is c0 times the corresponding normalized norm. Dividing the source trilinear inequalities by c0 multiplies each constant by c0^2 and preserves time powers, all three negative-norm slots, and uniformity in a,b and the inputs. These are the two asserted normalized Ctri bounds. For completeness, bounded multilinear extensions used here and below follow by approximating inputs in their controlling norms: expand a difference of multilinear outputs as a sum of terms each containing one input difference. The bounds make outputs Cauchy and their limits independent of the approximants, with the same bound. Sobolev Fourier truncations are dense by convergence of the defining weighted square sums, and deleting the zero mode preserves mean zero. This gives the constant-input extensions; for path inputs the same argument gives the extension to the closure of the stated initial domain in its controlling X norm, without assuming density in the entire X space."},{"id":"S002","text":"H002 gives 0<gamma<=1. Define a_gamma by the displayed three-case formula; the three cases give a_gamma in [0,1). First establish the required Sobolev comparison directly from D003: for real q<=r and any u in H^r, each weight (1+k^2)^q is at most (1+k^2)^r. Summing the weighted squared coefficients gives ||u||_(H^q)<=||u||_(H^r), with the identity inclusion on Fourier coefficients. For completions this follows first for Fourier truncations and then by their norm convergence; for the nonnegative exponents used here these inclusions identify the same L2 function. For 0<gamma<1/2 or 1/2<gamma<=1, E005 with sigma=gamma has source a(sigma)=a_gamma and directly gives the product inequality. At gamma=1/2, do not prescribe a numerical source epsilon. E005's 'any sufficiently small positive epsilon' means there is a positive threshold below which all positive choices are permitted; taking half the minimum of such a threshold and 1/8 supplies a permitted epsP with 0<epsP<=1/8. Fix one such epsP independently of f,g. Apply E005 with sigma=1/2 and source a(sigma)=epsP to arbitrary f in H^(1/2) and g in H^(1/8), using the just-proved inclusion H^(1/8) into H^epsP. Its exact conclusion is |f*g|_2<=c_(1/2,epsP)|f|_(1/2)|g|_(epsP), bounded by c_(1/2,epsP)|f|_(1/2)|g|_(1/8). Dividing every bilinear source inequality by c0 changes the constant by c0, and proves F002 with the unchanged a_gamma. The fixed source-dependent epsP is absorbed in C_gamma. No gamma=0 product assertion is needed."},{"id":"S003","text":"First fix a permitted epsF with 0<epsF<=1/8 for E004's sigma=1/2 case. Such a choice exists because E004 allows every sufficiently small fixed positive epsilon: choose half the minimum of a positive admissibility threshold and 1/8. Fix this choice using E004 alone, independently of all stage data; only the gamma=1/2 case uses it. Fix the arbitrary a,tau,t,w,M0 as declared and assume L1: ||w||_(H^gamma)<=M0. Set wshift=S_a w. The multiplier of S_a has modulus one and is one at frequency zero, so it preserves each H norm and the mean. Its multiplier group law gives B_(a+r)(f,g)=S_(-a)B_r(S_a f,S_a g) for smooth time-independent inputs. Substituting s=a+r in D021 and moving the fixed bounded map S_(-a) through the smooth time integral gives Fstage_(a,t)(w)=S_(-a)Fstage_(0,t-a)(wshift), initially for smooth mean-zero w. Apply E004 with sigma=gamma, n=0, timestep tau, s=t-a, f=g=wshift and beta=a_gamma; use epsF at gamma=1/2. Here tau>0 and 0<=t-a<=tau. For gamma<1/2, beta0=2*gamma-3/2 and a_gamma-beta0=2-3*gamma>0; for gamma=1/2, beta0=-1/2-epsF and a_gamma-beta0=5/8+epsF>0; for gamma>1/2 the gap is 1-gamma>=0. In every case a_gamma<=1+gamma. The denominator 1+gamma-beta0 is respectively 5/2-gamma, 2+epsF, and 2, all positive. Thus E004 yields exactly the source powers (1/2+2*gamma)/(5/2-gamma), (11/8)/(2+epsF), and (1+gamma)/2 in these three cases. Each is at least gamma: in the first case subtracting gamma times the positive denominator from the numerator gives 1/2-gamma/2+gamma^2>0; in the middle case epsF<=1/8 implies (11/8)/(2+epsF)>=11/17>1/2; in the last case 1>=gamma. Since 0<tau<=1, a power at least gamma gives tau to that power at most tau^gamma. Divide the source bilinear estimate by c0, multiplying its constant by c0. Use the isometry S_(-a) and ||wshift||_(H^gamma)=||w||_(H^gamma)<=M0 to obtain ||Fstage_(a,t)(w)||_(H^(a_gamma))<=C_gamma*M0^2*tau^gamma uniformly over a<=t<=a+tau. These calculations first hold on mean-zero trigonometric polynomials; E004 is bilinear, so the difference and completion argument in S001 extends its bounds and the covariance identity to mean-zero H^gamma inputs. To pass the covariance identity, apply it to Fourier truncations and use the bounded S maps on the source and target Sobolev spaces. All constants are independent of a,t,tau and Fourier support. Taking the supremum in t proves F003; the fixed epsF is absorbed in C_gamma."},{"id":"S004","text":"Apply E004 to the same wshift and time parameters with beta=1+gamma, retaining the permitted epsF at gamma=1/2. This is the upper endpoint of [beta0,1+gamma]. The positive denominator was checked in S003 and the numerator 1+gamma-beta is zero, so the source power is zero. After division by c0 the exact estimate is ||Fstage_(0,t-a)(wshift)||_(H^(1+gamma))<=C_gamma||wshift||_(H^gamma)^2. The smooth covariance identity established in S003 and the S isometry give the same bound for Fstage_(a,t)(w); the bilinear difference and Fourier completion argument of S001 extends both this bound and the identity to H^gamma inputs, now with target H^(1+gamma). Under L1 the right side is at most C_gamma*M0^2, uniformly in a,t,tau and Fourier support. Enlarge C_(gamma,M0) to cover this bound and F003 (for example a fixed C_gamma*(1+M0^2)). Discharge L1 into the norm-bound antecedent and universally quantify M0,w,tau,a,t. The two target Sobolev extensions agree as distributions because they arise from the same polynomial truncations and H^(1+gamma) includes continuously in H^(a_gamma) by the Fourier norm comparison proved in S002. With F001 and F002 this proves every clause of the output assertion, including the stated extensions. Neither chosen source epsilon occurs in the public input/output."}]}
{"source":{"path":"nl0.json","sha256":"b2e27be9cff9d51b56beb8118e64690a2142629470829eee3869eaa7aa3af7d5","background_mode":"node_local_reference","background_supplement":{"policy":"mathematical-background-v2-1","graph_sha256":"b2e27be9cff9d51b56beb8118e64690a2142629470829eee3869eaa7aa3af7d5","spec_sha256":"791c53dbac36e0a043c0e0e79679797dd4c0af6a24cd5c32dd0d594719e78229","notes":{"R001":[{"id":"BG001","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/1/text","fact":"For every real s, spatial reflection with sign Jf(x)=-f(-x) is an isometric involution on the mean-zero homogeneous and inhomogeneous Fourier Sobolev spaces, preserving reality. The multipliers S_t and the source untwisting multipliers exp(-it k^3) are strongly continuous isometries on these spaces. Thus a continuous twisted H^s path untwists to a continuous physical H^s path; J and these multipliers preserve convergence used in smooth approximation.","grounds":[{"location":"/definitions/1","reason":"The Fourier normalization specifies the reflection coefficient identity."},{"location":"/definitions/2","reason":"H^s is defined by the weighted square sum and reality by coefficient conjugacy."},{"location":"/definitions/3","reason":"The homogeneous norms are on the mean-zero subspace."},{"location":"/definitions/4","reason":"The Airy multiplier has unit modulus."},{"location":"/inputs/3/statement","reason":"E001 supplies continuity of the source twisted path at the applicable regularity."}],"justification":"Change variables x to -x modulo 2pi to obtain (Jf)^hat(k)=-f^hat(-k), first for polynomials and then by completion. The weights are even, which proves the isometry and real/mean-zero assertions. Multiplier continuity follows by approximating a fixed vector by a finite Fourier sum: its finite part is continuous in t and the remaining weighted square norm stays uniformly small by isometry. For a varying path split the difference into the path difference and the multiplier difference on a fixed vector. Apply these bounded maps to each approximating path; the same split proves continuity and preserves convergence."},{"id":"BG002","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/1/text","fact":"For U in C([0,T];L^2(Torus)), the distributional source equation U_t=UU_x+U_xxx, with UU_x=(1/2)partial_x(U^2), is equivalent to the coefficient integral equation stated in E001 for z_k(t)=exp(ik^3t) U^hat(t)(k). Such z is in L^infinity([0,T];dot H^0) when U is mean-zero. Reflection J transports this equation to u_t+u_xxx=(1/2)partial_x(u^2). Hence continuous L^2 solutions lie in the stated source uniqueness class after inverse reflection.","grounds":[{"location":"/inputs/3/statement","reason":"Supplies the precise source coefficient formulation and uniqueness class."},{"location":"/inference/argument/1/text","reason":"Uses reflection on the source distributional solution and invokes Fourier-mode testing for uniqueness."},{"location":"/definitions/1","reason":"Fixes the Fourier derivative signs and convolution normalization."},{"location":"/definitions/7","reason":"The target equation is distributional with quadratic term (1/2)partial_x(u^2)."}],"justification":"For each k, the convolution sum of U^hat(l)U^hat(k-l) converges absolutely by Cauchy-Schwarz and is the kth coefficient of U^2. This follows first for Fourier polynomials and then from their L^2 convergence, since ||f^2-g^2||_1 <= (||f||_2+||g||_2)||f-g||_2. Thus each nonlinear Fourier coefficient is continuous in time. Fourier testing gives d U^hat(k)/dt=-ik^3 U^hat(k)+(ik/2)sum_l U^hat(l)U^hat(k-l); multiplying by exp(ik^3t) and integrating yields exactly the phase exp(i3k l(k-l)t). Conversely these integrated identities imply the distributional equation, first for finite spatial Fourier tests and then for smooth tests by their Fourier convergence and the L^2/L^1 bounds. A continuous L^2 path is bounded on the compact time interval, and twisting preserves its norm. Reflection is a continuous change of variables on distributions, with the derivative signs computed in S002, so the same identities apply to the limiting solution and to any competing continuous L^2 solution."},{"id":"BG003","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/1/text","fact":"If every finite weighted Fourier square sum of a mean-zero path is at most C^2, with C>=0, its full homogeneous H^gamma norm is at most C. These finite-sum inequalities pass through coefficientwise convergence. An a.e.-in-time uniform Sobolev bound on a continuous Sobolev path extends to every time.","grounds":[{"location":"/inputs/3/statement","reason":"E001 supplies uniform Galerkin bounds and explicitly the same bound for its continuous limiting source solution."},{"location":"/inference/argument/1/text","reason":"The bound A is obtained from finite Fourier sums and the homogeneous/inhomogeneous comparison."},{"location":"/definitions/2","reason":"Sobolev square norms are sums of nonnegative weighted Fourier terms."},{"location":"/definitions/3","reason":"For gamma>0 the inhomogeneous norm is at most 2^(gamma/2) times the homogeneous norm on mean-zero inputs."}],"justification":"For each finite cutoff, coefficient convergence gives convergence of the finite sum. The full nonnegative series is the supremum over these increasing finite sums, so a common squared-norm upper bound persists. If a continuous norm exceeded its essential upper bound at any time, continuity would give a nonempty relative time interval of positive measure where it also exceeded that bound, a contradiction. Here E001 already gives the limiting bound; the finite-sum reasoning only explains the stated passage to it and introduces no additional compactness claim."},{"id":"BG004","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/2/text","fact":"A smooth periodic datum has finite H^m and dot H^m norms for every positive integer m. A periodic KdV path belonging to C([0,T];H^m) for every such m is smooth in space and time (with one-sided time derivatives at the endpoints). The smooth-path twist v=S_(-t)u is differentiable and satisfies v'=(1/2)B_t(v,v).","grounds":[{"location":"/inference/argument/2/text","reason":"L3 supplies smooth datum, and E001 is applied at every positive-integer regularity; uniqueness makes these the same path."},{"location":"/inputs/3/statement","reason":"Supplies continuity at each regularity and compatibility of source flows."},{"location":"/definitions/4","reason":"The Fourier multiplier of S_t has derivative ik^3 times that multiplier, corresponding to -partial_x^3."},{"location":"/definitions/6","reason":"B_t is defined on smooth inputs as S_(-t)partial_x[(S_t f)(S_t g)]."},{"location":"/definitions/7","reason":"The equation gives u_t=-u_xxx+(1/2)partial_x(u^2)."}],"justification":"Repeated integration by parts gives arbitrarily fast Fourier decay for smooth data. For a path in all H^m, Cauchy-Schwarz with m>j+1/2 gives absolute, uniformly convergent Fourier series for each jth spatial derivative; continuity in H^m gives joint continuity. Products are continuous in each integer H^m for m>=1: by the Leibniz rule, in every derivative term up to order m, one factor has derivative order at most m-1 and is bounded by its H^1 norm via the same Fourier Cauchy-Schwarz estimate, while the other is in L^2. The distributional equation consequently has a continuous right side in any desired H^r, using continuity of u in a sufficiently higher H^m. Integrating it in H^r gives a continuously differentiable time path there. Repeating this argument with the differentiated polynomial equation yields every time derivative and joint smoothness. Finally differentiating S_(-t) on these smooth paths gives v'=S_(-t)(u_t+u_xxx)=S_(-t)partial_x(u^2)/2=B_t(v,v)/2, exactly the identity in S002."}],"R002":[{"id":"BG001","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/0/text","fact":"For real a, the complex-valued function f_a(s)=exp(-i*a*s) on the real line is smooth, f_a'(s)=-i*a*f_a(s), |f_a(s)|=1, f_0(s)=1, and f_a(s)f_b(s)=f_(a+b)(s) for real b. For real r,s, |f_a(s)-f_a(r)|<=|a|*|s-r|. Consequently h(s)=f_alpha(s)-Mavg_tau(f_alpha) is smooth and |h'(s)|=|alpha|.","grounds":[{"location…8434 tokens truncated…matical","at":"/inference/argument/2/text","fact":"For smooth periodic h, ||h||_infinity<=C||h||_(H^1). In particular sup_I||S_t F(t)||_infinity is bounded by a constant depending only on gamma,M0 in S003.","grounds":[{"location":"/inputs/2/statement","reason":"The inverse-derivative estimate gives ||Dinv g||_infinity<=C||g||_2 for mean-zero g."},{"location":"/definitions/2/definition","reason":"The zero coefficient and derivative L^2 norm are bounded by the H^1 norm."},{"location":"/definitions/3/definition","reason":"S_t preserves H^1."},{"location":"/inference/argument/0/produces/0/statement","reason":"F001 already bounds sup_I||F||_(H^1) uniformly."}],"justification":"Take g=partial_x h, which is mean-zero. The Fourier identity h=hat h(0)+Dinv g gives ||h||_infinity<=|hat h(0)|+C||partial_x h||_2<=C'||h||_(H^1). Apply this to S_t F(t) and use its H^1 isometry. Thus the error product in S003 is bounded by C*delta, using the pointwise product inequality ||uv||_2<=||u||_2||v||_infinity."},{"id":"BG003","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/2/text","fact":"For these smooth data the interpolation paths are continuously differentiable with values in every fixed Sobolev space. For any continuous Banach-space-valued G on [a,b], U(t)=integral_a^t G(s)ds satisfies U'=G and ||U(t)||<=integral_a^t||G(s)||ds<=(t-a)*sup_[a,t]||G||. Bounded linear Fourier multipliers commute with these integrals. In particular the displayed derivatives of F,Cst,Q,Y and the integration of Dinv Y' from Y(a)=0 are valid.","grounds":[{"location":"/inference/locals/3/declaration","reason":"f is smooth."},{"location":"/inference/locals/4/declaration","reason":"w is smooth."},{"location":"/definitions/3/definition","reason":"On smooth functions, time derivatives of S_t multiply Fourier coefficients by powers of k^3."},{"location":"/definitions/9/definition","reason":"The smooth-input Rem formula has bounded eta and rapidly decreasing input coefficients."},{"location":"/definitions/12/definition","reason":"B is explicitly a smooth multiplier/product/derivative construction."},{"location":"/definitions/15/definition","reason":"Fstage is the time primitive of B/2."},{"location":"/definitions/16/definition","reason":"Defines Cst,Q,W,Y by finite operations and time integrals."}],"justification":"Rapid decay of smooth Fourier coefficients permits termwise spatial and time differentiation of the multiplier expressions, uniformly on compact time intervals. Products preserve smoothness. In Rem, the convolution of rapidly decreasing coefficients stays rapidly decreasing even after multiplication by any fixed power of the output frequency, since |eta|<=2 and |k|^(-1)<=1 off zero; thus W is spatially smooth. For continuous G, the difference quotient of its primitive is the average of G over a shrinking interval, which tends in norm to G(t) by continuity. The integral norm bound follows from triangle inequalities on Riemann sums, and bounded linear maps pass through their norm limits. Applying these facts to D022 gives all derivatives and initial values used in S001-S004, without any approximation of rough data. Zero-length integrals at t=a vanish, so the Ctri estimates there require no convention for 0^0."}],"R009":[{"id":"BG001","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/0/text","fact":"For a smooth Sobolev-valued path G on [a,c], K(t)=integral_a^t G(s)ds is differentiable with K'=G, K(a)=0 and ||K(t)||<=integral_a^t||G(s)||ds. These facts apply to the integrands defining F, Cst, Q and rho on the current smooth step.","grounds":[{"location":"/definitions/19/definition","reason":"Interp is formed from time integrals of B applied to smooth step data and their stages."},{"location":"/definitions/15/definition","reason":"B is defined by smooth Fourier evolution, multiplication and a spatial derivative on smooth inputs."},{"location":"/definitions/12/definition","reason":"Rem on smooth inputs is defined by absolutely convergent rapidly decreasing Fourier series and is fixed on each step."}],"justification":"For continuous Banach-space-valued G, the difference between a difference quotient of its integral and G(t) is bounded by the supremum of ||G(s)-G(t)|| on the shrinking interval; hence it tends to zero. The integral norm inequality follows from triangle inequalities for Riemann sums and their limits. Smooth spatial data remain smooth under the Fourier multipliers here: any fixed number of space/time derivatives introduces only polynomial frequency factors, absorbed by rapid Fourier decay. Finite products and these time integrals preserve smoothness."},{"id":"BG002","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/1/text","fact":"For J=[a,t] contained in K=[a,a+tau], X(K) bounds restrict to X(J) with no larger norm. A constant-in-time L2 function f has weak derivative zero and ||f||_(X(J))<=||f||_2. Also ||f||_2<=||f||_(H^gamma) when gamma>=0.","grounds":[{"location":"/definitions/14/definition","reason":"X is the sum of the L-infinity L2 norm and the L-infinity H^(-pstar) derivative norm."},{"location":"/inputs/4/statement","reason":"The needed Y and z norms are supplied on the full step; f is a fixed L2 step datum."},{"location":"/inputs/0/statement","reason":"gamma is positive."},{"location":"/definitions/2/definition","reason":"Sobolev norms use Fourier weights (1+k^2)^gamma."}],"justification":"Restriction cannot increase essential suprema and preserves the weak derivative. A constant path has zero distributional derivative. Each Fourier weight is at least one for gamma>=0, so comparison of the nonnegative sums gives the Sobolev-to-L2 inequality. These facts justify N006 on initial subintervals and the norm comparisons later in S005."},{"id":"BG003","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/3/text","fact":"A continuous path obtained by concatenating finitely many smooth pieces has distributional derivative equal to its piecewise derivative, with no point masses at joins. If each piecewise derivative is bounded in H^(-pstar) by K, the weak derivative belongs to L-infinity H^(-pstar) with norm at most K.","grounds":[{"location":"/inputs/3/statement","reason":"N007 supplies a finite continuous piecewise smooth numerical interpolation with matching grid values."},{"location":"/inference/argument/2/produces/0/statement","reason":"Each residual increment starts at zero and has its H^(-pstar) derivative bounded."},{"location":"/inference/argument/3/text","reason":"Rres is constructed by adding each new step increment to the preceding endpoint; v is smooth."},{"location":"/definitions/14/definition","reason":"X uses weak derivatives and essential suprema."}],"justification":"Integrate each smooth piece against a compactly supported scalar test-function derivative. Integration by parts gives endpoint terms that cancel pairwise by continuity. The remaining integral is the pairing with the piecewise derivative. Finite joins have measure zero, so its essential supremum is at most the maximum piecewise bound. This applies to V, Rres and e=v-V."}],"R010":[{"id":"BG001","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/0/text","fact":"For a continuous path u on a compact interval which is continuously differentiable on each member of a finite partition, with values in a Sobolev Hilbert space, u(t)-u(a)=integral_a^t u'(s) ds. For an integrable Hilbert-valued path F, ||integral_J F||<=integral_J ||F||. A bounded linear operator commutes with this integral. These assertions apply to the smooth spatial paths and continuous piecewise smooth time paths here; identities can be established on each smooth segment and summed.","grounds":[{"location":"/inference/locals","reason":"v is smooth and V is continuous piecewise smooth; e=v-V and g=v-e/2 inherit this regularity."},{"location":"/inputs/4","reason":"The continuous concatenated residual and its stepwise derivative are provided by N009."},{"location":"/definitions/2","reason":"The spatial normed codomains are the specified Sobolev Hilbert spaces."}],"justification":"The fundamental theorem for a continuously differentiable Hilbert-valued path follows by integrating its derivative on each smooth piece; continuity cancels adjacent endpoint values. The integral norm inequality follows first for step functions by the triangle inequality and then by integral approximation. Bounded linear maps pass through the same approximation. The compact interval meets only finitely many steps since tau>0. These facts justify integration of the error equation, projection of integrals, and summation across step endpoints without any exchange of a singular limit."},{"id":"BG002","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/3/text","fact":"For the supplied Fourier norms and projections, Ple_R and Pgt_R contract H^s for every real s and commute with time differentiation. Consequently they contract X(I). Restriction to a subinterval also contracts both components of X. For N>=2, ||Pgt_N A||_2<=N^(-1)||A||_(H^1), ||Ple_N partial_x^2 A||_2<=N^2||A||_2, and ||Pgt_(N/3) f||_(H^(-pstar))<=3^pstar N^(-pstar)||f||_2. Moreover Pgt_N Ctri_J(Ple_(N/3) f_1,Ple_(N/3) f_2,Ple_(N/3) f_3)=0 on any time subinterval J.","grounds":[{"location":"/definitions/2","reason":"H^s norms are the specified weighted squared Fourier sums."},{"location":"/definitions/3","reason":"S_t multiplies individual Fourier modes without changing their indices."},{"location":"/definitions/6","reason":"Dinv acts diagonally and does not enlarge Fourier support."},{"location":"/definitions/7","reason":"Ple and Pgt are the stated sharp spectral cutoffs."},{"location":"/definitions/11","reason":"pstar=23/14>0."},{"location":"/definitions/12","reason":"X is the sum of a spatial L^2 supremum and a weak-derivative H^(-pstar) essential supremum."},{"location":"/definitions/15","reason":"Ctri is formed from products, diagonal multipliers and mean-zero projections."}],"justification":"Deleting terms contracts a nonnegative weighted Fourier sum. On |k|>N, (1+k^2)^(-1/2)<=N^(-1); on |k|<=N, k^2<=N^2; on |k|>N/3, (1+k^2)^(-pstar/2)<=3^pstar N^(-pstar). These give the three inequalities by squaring and summing. Fixed bounded Fourier projections commute with weak time derivatives, and restricting a supremum or essential supremum cannot increase it. Each low input has finite Fourier support |k|<=N/3, and multiplication adds indices; neither P, S nor Dinv adds indices. Thus the all-low trilinear integrand and its time integral have support |k|<=N. This is precisely the routine Fourier content used in S002-S004."},{"id":"BG003","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/4/text","fact":"On intervals of positive length, the supplied X norm satisfies the triangle inequality, and its L^infinity L^2 component bounds the spatial L^2 norm at every time for continuous paths. On every step segment J contained in I, restrictions of v,V,g have the same upper X bounds as on I. Also ||J_s(A,G)||_2<=C||A||_2||G||_2 is a direct consequence of N005's H^1 bound. In particular if |J|<=tau, ||W_n||_2<=C tau q_n and sup_J||V||_2<=C, then ||tau^(-1) integral_J J_s(W_n,V(s)) ds||_2<=C tau q_n. A segment of zero length has zero integral.","grounds":[{"location":"/definitions/12","reason":"X is a sum of the two stated norms, with continuous representatives for the paths."},{"location":"/inputs/1/statement","reason":"N005 supplies ||J_s(A,G)||_(H^1)<=C||A||_2||G||_2 uniformly in time."},{"location":"/inputs/3/statement","reason":"N008 supplies ||W_n||_2<=C tau q_n."},{"location":"/inference/argument/0/produces/0/statement","reason":"F001 supplies the uniform X bounds for v,V,g."},{"location":"/inference/argument/4/text","reason":"The integrals are over intersections of [a,t] with steps, so each length is at most tau>0."}],"justification":"Triangle inequalities hold for both norm components and for their sum. A continuous norm exceeding an essential upper bound at an interior point would exceed it on a set of positive measure; endpoints follow by continuity. The Fourier weights defining H^1 dominate the L^2 weights, so N005 gives the stated L^2 J bound. The integral norm inequality then yields tau^(-1)|J| C||W_n||_2 sup_J||V||_2<=C tau q_n. This confirms the forcing term also on a partial final step; no derivative bound for W_n is required."}],"R011":[{"id":"BG001","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/3/text","fact":"For the Fourier multiplier S_t of D005 and every real s, S_t is a linear isometry on H^s, S_t S_r=S_(t+r), and t -> S_t f is continuous in H^s for each f in H^s. Consequently, the N001 solution u in C([0,T];H^gamma) has a continuous twist v(t)=S_(-t)u(t); since gamma>0 it is also L2-continuous. Subtracting the N007 continuous interpolation gives an L2-continuous e, with e(0)=0. The same isometry gives ||S_(-n*tau)(u(n*tau)-u^n)||_2=||u(n*tau)-u^n||_2.","grounds":[{"location":"/definitions/2/definition","reason":"H^s is the weighted square-sum completion, and L2=H^0."},{"location":"/definitions/4/definition","reason":"The precise multiplier is exp(i*t*k^3)."},{"location":"/inputs/1/statement","reason":"gamma>0."},{"location":"/inputs/3/statement","reason":"The exact solution is H^gamma-continuous with the stated initial value."},{"location":"/inputs/4/statement","reason":"The numerical interpolation is continuous and matches the grid values."},{"location":"/definitions/10/definition","reason":"u^0=u_0 and v^0=S_0 u_0."}],"justification":"Each multiplier has modulus one and its phases multiply by addition of times, proving the norm and group identities first on polynomials and then on the completion. For strong continuity approximate f by a polynomial: the two tail terms are bounded by twice the approximation error, and the finitely many polynomial coefficients depend continuously on time. For varying u(t), add and subtract S_(-t)u(t0) and use the isometry. The weight (1+k^2)^gamma>=1 gives ||f||_2<=||f||_(H^gamma). Difference continuity, S_0=I and initial endpoint matching give continuity and e(0)=0. The grid identity follows by linearity and isometry."},{"id":"BG002","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/2/text","fact":"For gamma>0, positive real powers are increasing and (x^(1/gamma))^gamma=x for x>0. With the positive Csm,delta0 chosen in S003, tausm=min(1,delta0/2,(1/(4*(Csm+1)))^(1/gamma)) is positive, and 0<tau<=tausm implies Csm*tau^gamma<=Csm/(4*(Csm+1))<1/4.","grounds":[{"location":"/inputs/1/statement","reason":"gamma>0."},{"location":"/inference/argument/1/produces/0/statement","reason":"delta0>0 and K0>0 are available jointly."},{"location":"/inference/argument/2/text","reason":"Bcount is a positive integer and Csm=K0*delta0*(2^Bcount-1)>0."}],"justification":"For positive x, x^gamma=exp(gamma*log(x)); monotonicity and inverse identities for log and exp give the stated power facts. Every entry of the finite minimum is positive. Raising tau<= (1/(4*(Csm+1)))^(1/gamma) to gamma yields the claimed estimate, whose strict inequality follows from Csm/(Csm+1)<1."},{"id":"BG003","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/4/text","fact":"If e:[0,T]->L2 is continuous, T>0 and e(0)=0, then g(t)=||e(t)||_2 is continuous and has a maximum on every compact initial interval. If g reaches 1, there is a first time t*>0 with g(t*)=1 and g(t)<=1 for 0<=t<=t*. If it never reaches 1, then g(t)<1 everywhere and max_[0,T] g<1.","grounds":[{"location":"/inputs/0/statement","reason":"T>0."},{"location":"/inference/argument/3/text","reason":"The paths constructed from N001 and N007 give continuous e and e(0)=0, as checked in BG001."},{"location":"/inference/argument/4/text","reason":"L2 temporarily assumes the level 1 is attained."}],"justification":"The reverse triangle inequality makes the norm continuous. A continuous real function on a compact interval attains its extrema. Under L2 its nonempty level set at 1 is closed and compact, hence has a least time, positive because g(0)=0. Any earlier value greater than 1 would produce an earlier value 1 by the intermediate value theorem. If the level set is empty, the same theorem rules out values above 1; compactness then makes the attained maximum strictly less than 1. These facts justify exactly the displayed first-time bootstrap contradiction."}],"R012":[{"id":"BG001","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/1/text","fact":"For gamma>0 and a real mean-zero f in H^gamma(Torus), the symmetric Fourier truncations f_m=Ple_m f, m positive integer, are smooth real mean-zero trigonometric polynomials with ||f_m||_(H^gamma)<=||f||_(H^gamma). Moreover ||f||_2<=||f||_(H^gamma), ||f_m||_2<=||f||_(H^gamma), and f_m tends to f in H^gamma and hence in L2.","grounds":[{"location":"/inputs/1/statement","reason":"Gives gamma>0."},{"location":"/inference/argument/1/text","reason":"L1 supplies the real mean-zero datum in H^gamma with norm at most R and defines its truncations."},{"location":"/definitions/2/definition","reason":"Gives the weighted Fourier norm, its completion, and the meanings of real and mean-zero."},{"location":"/definitions/11/definition","reason":"Gives symmetric finite-frequency projection coefficients."}],"justification":"The retained frequencies form a finite set, so their inverse Fourier sum is smooth. The symmetric indicator preserves coefficient conjugacy and the zero coefficient. Removing nonnegative terms contracts the squared H^gamma norm. Since (1+k^2)^gamma>=1, the L2 norm is dominated by that norm (and this bounded inclusion extends to the completion). The squared H^gamma approximation error is the tail sum over |k|>m of the convergent nonnegative weighted Fourier series, which tends to zero; L2 convergence follows by domination."},{"id":"BG002","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/2/text","fact":"If x_m tends to x in a normed subspace X and F:X->X is continuous, then for any fixed nonnegative integer j, F iterated j times at x_m tends to F iterated j times at x. In this packet X is the real mean-zero L2 space and F=Psi_tau for the fixed tau>0.","grounds":[{"location":"/inference/argument/1/text","reason":"Supplies convergent initial truncations and tau=T/Lsteps>0 under L1."},{"location":"/inputs/4/statement","reason":"Psi_tau is continuous on X and preserves X."},{"location":"/definitions/10/definition","reason":"Identifies the numerical trajectories with finite iterations of the same Psi_tau."}],"justification":"For j=0 this is the initial convergence. If convergence holds at j, continuity at F iterated j times at x sends that convergent sequence to the required limit at j+1, by the epsilon-delta definition of continuity. Induction supplies every j up to the fixed finite Lsteps; preservation ensures each continuity application lies in X."},{"id":"BG003","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/3/text","fact":"In a normed space, if a_m^n tends to a^n and b_m^n tends to b^n for every n in a fixed finite nonempty index set, and ||a_m^n-b_m^n||<=B for every m,n with fixed real B, then max_n ||a^n-b^n||<=B.","grounds":[{"location":"/inference/argument/1/produces/0/statement","reason":"F002 provides the common bound B=C*tau^gamma for all approximating trajectories."},{"location":"/inference/argument/2/produces/0/statement","reason":"F003 provides both convergences at every grid point."},{"location":"/inference/argument/1/text","reason":"L1 fixes a positive integer Lsteps, so n=0,...,Lsteps is a finite nonempty set."}],"justification":"The triangle inequality gives | ||a_m^n-b_m^n||-||a^n-b^n|| | <= ||a_m^n-a^n||+||b_m^n-b^n||, whose right side tends to zero. The limit therefore remains <=B at each n: a larger limit would contradict convergence with epsilon half its excess over B. Taking the maximum of the finitely many resulting inequalities yields the assertion."}]},"sha256":"bdd658d195f9c9eca86ad4edd48304cfdb7b650a772b6e00eca43cd7cd962070"},"background_supplement_sha256":"bdd658d195f9c9eca86ad4edd48304cfdb7b650a772b6e00eca43cd7cd962070"},"stepsIdentical":true,"rootStatementsMatch":true,"stepCount":52}

